data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":2,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Use a **canary or one production replica** first—heap snapshots can pause the process, consume substantial memory/disk, and contain sensitive data.\n\n1. **Track memory over time:** log `process.memoryUsage()` (`heapUsed`, `heapTotal`, `external`, `arrayBuffers`, `rss`) alongside request volume, traffic mix, GC activity, and container limits. A steadily rising post-GC baseline is more suspicious than memory that rises with traffic and later falls.\n2. **Identify which memory grows:** rising `heapUsed` suggests retained JavaScript objects; rising `external`/`arrayBuffers` points toward buffers or native allocations; rising RSS alone can also reflect allocator behavior or fragmentation.\n3. **Capture two heap snapshots** under comparable load, separated by enough time for the suspected leak to accumulate. Compare them in Chrome DevTools **Memory**; inspect growing object types, retained size, dominators, and retainer paths to find what keeps objects alive. Use a secure, authenticated path—never expose the inspector publicly—and protect/delete snapshots carefully.\n4. **Check likely retainers:** unbounded caches or maps, listeners not removed, timers/intervals, closures holding request data, queued work, and buffers/native addons. Fix the owner/reference, then confirm memory plateaus under the same workload.\n5. **If snapshots are too risky,** start with heap/GC metrics or a sampling allocation profiler, and reproduce under realistic load in staging.\n\nAvoid restarting before collecting useful evidence if possible; set alerts and a rollback/restart threshold in case the canary approaches its memory limit."}}

